      "note": "IMPORTANT FINDING: the presence of *any* token string (even an obviously fake/invalid one) is sufficient to render the full password-reset form. There is no client-side token validity check. Invalid/expired tokens are only rejected server-side when the form is submitted (authClient.resetPassword throws -> handleAuthError -> toast.error). This is graceful (no crash), matching the expected behavior described in the task notes, but it does mean a user can land on and interact with a fully-populated reset form using a dead link, only discovering the problem after submitting. Not a crash/bug, but worth a test case: 'submit reset form with invalid token surfaces a toast error, not a silent failure.'"
